From: claude-opus-5 <noreply@local>
Date: 2026-11-02
Subject: [PATCH] aorus20 (0038) -- drop the pci_lock_rescan_remove() nesting around pci_reset_bus()

Removes one of two deadlock cycles in the M-recover work handler.

THE CYCLE THIS PATCH CLOSES. The handler wrapped its bus reset in the global
PCI rescan/remove lock:

    pci_lock_rescan_remove();
    rc = pci_reset_bus(bridge);
    pci_unlock_rescan_remove();

On a Thunderbolt hotplug the PCI core already holds pci_rescan_remove_lock
while it runs the remove path, and that path reaches nv_pci_remove_helper ->
tb_egpu_lever_m_recover_stop -> cancel_work_sync(&lm->reset_work), which waits
for this handler to return. The handler then asked for the very lock the
remover holds. AB-BA: the hotplug path wedges with the global rescan/remove
lock held, and on this machine a hard hang loses the journal (it lives in RAM),
so the result is also near-undebuggable.

Reachable whenever recovery is in flight and the box is unplugged or the module
is removed -- i.e. exactly during the failure the lever exists to handle.

WHY REMOVING IT IS RIGHT, not just convenient: pci_reset_bus() takes
pci_bus_lock() itself, and in-tree callers (vfio-pci among them) invoke it
without holding pci_rescan_remove_lock. The nesting was adding a lock the API
does not ask for.

THE CYCLE THIS PATCH DOES NOT CLOSE, stated plainly so nobody reads this as
"the deadlock is fixed": pci_reset_bus() -> pci_bus_lock() -> device_lock(gpu),
while the remove path holds device_lock(gpu) and waits in cancel_work_sync().
That cycle needs no global lock at all and survives this patch. Closing it
means the work item's lifetime must stop depending on a synchronous cancel
under device_lock -- a refcount on lm instead of the kfree() in _stop, or
moving the reset out of the work item entirely. That is a redesign of the
lifetime model, and a wrong refcount in a PCIe error path is a use-after-free,
so it is not something to write blind against a driver that cannot currently be
compiled. Options and trade-offs are in docs/research/open-driver-defects.md.

Net effect: two deadlock cycles become one, with no change to object lifetimes
and no new state.

NOT changed: the reset itself, the settle msleep, the 0027 explicit
slot_reset/resume dispatch, the refcount on pdev_for_work (which has its own
leak on a cancelled-before-run work item -- also written up, also not fixed
here).

Verified: applies in order on top of 0001-0037 over the kernel-open half of
595.99.02 (ci/40-kernel-patches.sh, section 7b). NOT compiled and NOT run on
hardware.
---
diff --git a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
index dde71ea..0db4306 100644
--- a/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
+++ b/kernel-open/nvidia/nv-lever-m-recover.c
@@ -253,10 +253,33 @@ static void tb_egpu_lever_m_reset_work_handler(struct work_struct *work)
 
     tb_egpu_lever_m_emit_uevent(pdev, "RECOVERING");
 
-    /* Serialise vs the kernel's PCI rescan/remove paths. */
-    pci_lock_rescan_remove();
+    /* Patch 0038: NO pci_lock_rescan_remove() around this call.
+     *
+     * It used to wrap the reset, to "serialise vs the kernel's PCI
+     * rescan/remove paths". That nesting created a deadlock: on a Thunderbolt
+     * hotplug the PCI core already holds pci_rescan_remove_lock while it runs
+     * the remove path, and that path reaches nv_pci_remove_helper ->
+     * tb_egpu_lever_m_recover_stop -> cancel_work_sync(&lm->reset_work), which
+     * waits for THIS handler. The handler then asked for the same global lock
+     * the remover is holding: AB-BA, with the PCI hotplug path wedged and the
+     * global rescan/remove lock held. On this machine a hard hang loses the
+     * journal, so it would also be near-undebuggable.
+     *
+     * Dropping the nesting is also what upstream callers do: pci_reset_bus()
+     * takes pci_bus_lock() itself, and in-tree users (vfio-pci among them)
+     * call it without holding pci_rescan_remove_lock. We were adding a lock
+     * the API does not ask for.
+     *
+     * A SECOND deadlock cycle survives this patch and is NOT fixed here:
+     * pci_reset_bus() -> pci_bus_lock() -> device_lock(gpu), while the remove
+     * path holds device_lock(gpu) and waits in cancel_work_sync(). Closing
+     * that one needs the work item's lifetime to stop depending on a
+     * synchronous cancel under device_lock -- a refcount on lm instead of the
+     * kfree() in _stop -- which is a redesign, not an ordering tweak, and a
+     * wrong refcount here is a use-after-free in a PCIe error path. It is
+     * written up in docs/research/open-driver-defects.md with the options;
+     * this patch removes one of the two cycles without touching lifetimes. */
     rc = pci_reset_bus(bridge);
-    pci_unlock_rescan_remove();
 
     /* Settle before any verification; gives the link time to retrain. */
     msleep(NVreg_TbEgpuLeverMResetSettleMs);
